sec.p:
To improve Dc prediction accuracy, Davis, John Alan et al. proposed an empirical formula:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$Dc=1.4G{\varepsilon }^{0.48}$$\end{document}Dc=1.4Gε0.48where G = Gx = Gy, ε = Δλ/λ, and λ = λx = λy. While this formula is relatively accurate for circular micropillar arrays with equal longitudinal and lateral periodic distance24, it fails for non-circular or unevenly spaced micropillars.

The mechanism of altered zigzag formation is as follows: The DLD structure can be regarded as a channel with alternating expansions and contractions, which induces secondary flows. Ignoring gravitational effects, slight pressure differences exist along the height of the channel, influencing the vertical motion of particles. Additionally, the fluid viscosity also affects particle motion in the vertical direction. The drag force acting on the particle includes both viscous and pressure-induced resistance, whose general expression can be written as:2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\,{{F}}_{{D}}=\frac{1}{2}{\rho }_{{\rm{sol}}}{v}^{2}{C}_{D}A$$\end{document}FD=12ρsolv2CDAwhere, FD represents the drag force, ρsol is the solution density, v denotes the relative velocity between the particle and the fluid, CD is the drag coefficient, and A is the projected area in the direction of motion. When considering only drag, the particle moves along the flow direction.

This remapping is implemented using the parallelogram rule of vectors, as illustrated in Fig. 6c. Let the coordinates of the four points be A, B, C, and D, and the center position of the particle be point P. Define the vectors CB = B − C, CD = D − C, and CP = P − C. According to the parallelogram rule, the position of P can be expressed as:3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${CP}=\alpha \cdot CD+\eta \cdot CB$$\end{document}CP=α⋅CD+η⋅CB

This can be solved using matrix operations:4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left[\begin{array}{l}\alpha \\ \eta \end{array}\right]={\left[\begin{array}{l}{\boldsymbol{C}}{\boldsymbol{D}}\\ {\boldsymbol{C}}{\boldsymbol{B}}\end{array}\right]}^{-T}\times {[{\boldsymbol{C}}{\boldsymbol{P}}]}^{T}$$\end{document}[αη]=[CDCB]−T×[CP]T

The particle coordinates are then remapped based on the values of α and η. If 0 ≤ α ≤ 1, the particle lies within the left and right boundaries of the parallelogram; if α < 0, the particle is to the left of the parallelogram; if α > 1, it is to the right. The remapping formula in the CD direction is:5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left\{\begin{array}{l}P=P\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\le \alpha \le 1\\ P=P+(D-C)\,\alpha < 0\\ P=P-(D-C)\,\alpha > 1\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}P=P0≤α≤1P=P+(D−C)α<0P=P−(D−C)α>1

Similarly, for the CB direction: If 0 ≤ η ≤ 1, the particle lies within the top and bottom boundaries of the parallelogram; if η < 0, it is below; if η > 1, it is above. The remapping formula is:6\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left\{\begin{array}{l}P=P\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\le \eta \le 1\\ P=P+(B-C)\,\eta < 0\\ P=P-(B-C)\,\eta > 1\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}{P=P0≤η≤1P=P+(B−C)η<0P=P−(B−C)η>1

Due to the viscosity of the solution, the physical field inside the channel is non-uniform, which results in varying physical quantities acting on different positions of the particle surface. To more accurately obtain the net physical quantities acting on the particle, the surface of the sphere is discretized using a finite element approach, as illustrated in Fig. 6d. The discretization method is as follows: First, set the current center of the particle as the origin of the coordinate system. Then, sliced into M equally thick layers along the Z-axis. Next, divide each layer into N equal-angle areas in the XY plane. This process results in a total of M × N small curved surface elements. Based on geometric relationships, the center coordinates (x, y, z) of each small surface element are calculated as:7\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{l}{x}=\sqrt{{R}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\cdot cos\frac{(2\cdot n-1)\cdot \pi }{N}\\ y=\sqrt{{R}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\cdot sin\frac{(2\cdot n-1)\cdot \pi }{N}\\ z=\frac{M+1-2\cdot m}{M}\cdot R\end{array}$$\end{document}x=R2−z2⋅cos(2⋅n−1)⋅πNy=R2−z2⋅sin(2⋅n−1)⋅πNz=M+1−2⋅mM⋅Rwhere, m is the index of the spherical slice, n is the index of the angular sector, and R is the radius of the sphere. According to the spherical cap area formula39, the area of each small surface element is:8\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${S}_{scf}=\frac{4\pi {R}^{2}}{M\cdot N}$$\end{document}Sscf=4πR2M⋅Nwhere, the subscript scf stands for small curved face, indicating that each surface element is assigned an equal weight. This uniform weighting facilitates subsequent calculations of the particle velocity and pressure.

The barycentric coordinate method is first used to identify the tetrahedral element that contains a given point P. As shown in Fig. 6e, let the coordinates of the four vertices of a tetrahedron be H, I, J, and K, and the coordinate of the center point of a small surface element be P, all in the form of (x, y, z). The calculation method is:9\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$P={\gamma }_{H}H+{\gamma }_{I}I+{\gamma }_{J}J+{\gamma }_{K}K$$\end{document}P=γHH+γII+γJJ+γKK10\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left[\begin{array}{l}{\gamma }_{H}\\ {\gamma }_{I}\\ {\gamma }_{J}\\ {\gamma }_{K}\end{array}\right]={\left[\begin{array}{l}H,1\\ I,1\\ J,1\\ K,1\end{array}\right]}^{-T}\times {\left[P,1\right]}^{T}$$\end{document}γHγIγJγK=H,1I,1J,1K,1−T×P,1T

Here, γH, γI, γJ, and γK are the weights corresponding to the vertices H, I, J, and K, respectively. A necessary and sufficient condition for point P to lie within the tetrahedron is that all four γ values are greater than or equal to zero. Next, the physical quantity at point P is estimated using the values and weights at the four vertices. Let VH, VI, VJ, and VK be the values of the physical quantity (e.g., velocity or pressure) at points H, I, J, and K, respectively. Then the physical quantity at point P is calculated by:11\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${V}_{P}={\gamma }_{H}{V}_{H}+{\gamma }_{I}{V}_{I}+{\gamma }_{J}{V}_{J}+{\gamma }_{K}{V}_{K}$$\end{document}VP=γHVH+γIVI+γJVJ+γKVK

Due to the viscosity of the fluid, the surface of a particle immersed in it is subjected to a viscous force τ:12\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\boldsymbol{\tau }}=\mu \nabla {\boldsymbol{V}}\cdot S$$\end{document}τ=μ∇V⋅Swhere ∇V denotes the velocity gradient, and S is the area. In this method, it is assumed that the physical quantities on the particle surface are equal to those of the flow field at the corresponding positions. However, since the velocity gradient inside the DLD device varies spatially, the viscous forces acting on different points of the particle surface differ accordingly. Given that the particle is a rigid body, these non-uniform viscous forces lead to both rotational and translational movement, see Video S1 and Fig. S9.

As shown in Fig. 6f, let point O be the center of the particle and point P be the center of a small surface element, with the radial vector defined as OP. Assume the velocity VP on the surface of the sphere and the fluid velocity VO at the center are obtained by (11). According to geometric relations, the projection of the difference between VP and Vavg onto OP is:13\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{rad}}}\,=\frac{({{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{P}}}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{O}}})\cdot {\boldsymbol{OP}}}{{\Vert {\boldsymbol{OP}}\Vert }^{2}}\,\cdot {\boldsymbol{OP}}$$\end{document}Vrad=(VP−VO)⋅OP‖OP‖2⋅OP

Following vector decomposition rules, the tangential velocity component is:14\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{\tan }\,=({{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{P}}}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{O}}})-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{rad}}}\,$$\end{document}Vtan=(VP−VO)−Vrad

As described in ‘Surface discretization of the particle’ section, the area of each small surface element is the same, meaning the weights are equal. Therefore, the net radial velocity Vrad acting externally on the particle and the net tangential velocity Vtan on the particle surface are computed as averages:15\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${V}_{rad\_net}=\frac{1}{M\times N}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{m=1}^{M}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{n=1}^{N}{V}_{rad\_mn}+{V}_{{\boldsymbol{O}}}$$\end{document}Vrad_net=1M×N∑m=1M∑n=1NVrad_mn+VO16\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${V}_{tan\_net}=\frac{1}{M\times N}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{m=1}^{M}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{n=1}^{N}{V}_{tan\_mn}$$\end{document}Vtan_net=1M×N∑m=1M∑n=1NVtan_mnwhere the subscript mn indicates the index of each small surface element. It is worth noting that Vtan_net induces particle rotation, leading to a Magnus force40. However, the force generated by the Magnus effect is much smaller than the drag force and can thus be neglected in terms of its impact on displacement. On the other hand, Vrad_net contributes to particle translation.

Since Stokes’ formula only considers the drag force on an object caused by fluid viscosity under low Reynolds number conditions (Re ≪ 1)41, the viscous force Fvis acting on the particle can be approximated using the Stokes equation:17\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${F}_{vis}={F}_{stoke}=6\pi \mu R\cdot \times ({V}_{rad\_net}-{V}_{ps})$$\end{document}Fvis=Fstoke=6πμR·×(Vrad_net-Vps)where the Vps is the velocity of the particle

In ‘Obtaining velocity and pressure on the particle surface’ section, the pressure pscf at the center of each small surface element was obtained. This pressure value can be regarded as the average pressure over that small surface. According to the pressure formula, the pressure force Fscf acting on the small surface is:18\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${F}_{scf}={p}_{scf}\times {S}_{scf}$$\end{document}Fscf=pscf×Sscfthe pressure on each small surface element is projected through its center point to the center of the particle, and decomposed into pressure components Fx, Fy, and Fz.19\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{l}{F}_{x}=-\mathop{\sum }\limits_{m=1}^{M}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{n=1}^{N}{F}_{sc{f}_{mn}}\cdot sin({\theta }_{m})\cdot cos({\phi }_{n})\\ {F}_{y}=-\mathop{\sum }\limits_{m=1}^{M}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{n=1}^{N}{F}_{sc{f}_{mn}}\cdot sin({\theta }_{m})\cdot sin({\phi }_{n})\\ {F}_{z}=-\mathop{\sum }\limits_{m=1}^{M}\mathop{\sum }\limits_{n=1}^{N}{F}_{sc{f}_{mn}}\cdot cos({\theta }_{m})\end{array}$$\end{document}Fx=−∑m=1M∑n=1NFscfmn⋅sin(θm)⋅cos(ϕn)Fy=−∑m=1M∑n=1NFscfmn⋅sin(θm)⋅sin(ϕn)Fz=−∑m=1M∑n=1NFscfmn⋅cos(θm)where m represents the index of the layer in the Z direction, n represents the index in the angular direction, θ denotes the angle between the line connecting the center of the small surface and the particle center with respect to the Z-axis. ϕ denotes the angle between the projection of the pressure vector in the XOY-plane and the positive X-axis. As a result, the net pressure force Fpress acting on the suspended particle can be expressed in vector form as:20\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{{\boldsymbol{press}}}=({F}_{x},{F}_{y},{F}_{z})$$\end{document}Fpress=(Fx,Fy,Fz)

When a particle is located in a flow field with a significant velocity gradient, it inevitably experiences a fluid-induced lift force. This lift force can affect the particle trajectory36. Two types of lift forces are considered: one is the wall-induced lift caused by wall effects, and the other is the shear-induced lift. For the wall-induced lift, we adopt the simplified expression proposed by the team of Ho, B. P42., with the calculation formula given as:21\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{l}{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{{\boldsymbol{wL}}}={\rho }_{sol}\frac{{r}_{p}^{4}}{{D}^{2}}{|D({\boldsymbol{N}}\cdot \nabla ){{\boldsymbol{u}}}_{p}|}^{2}{G}_{1}(s){\boldsymbol{N}}\\ {{\boldsymbol{u}}}_{p}=({\boldsymbol{I}}-({\boldsymbol{N}}\otimes {\boldsymbol{N}})){{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{\boldsymbol{pf}}}\end{array}$$\end{document}FwL=ρsolrp4D2|D(N⋅∇)up|2G1(s)Nup=(I−(N⊗N))Vpfwhere ρsol is the density of the solution, rp is the radius of the particle, Vpf is the fluid velocity, I is the identity matrix, D is the distance between the channel walls, N is the normal vector of the reference wall, s is the ratio of the particle’s distance from the reference wall to D, and G1 is functions of s. The shear-induced lift is calculated using the classical Saffman lift formula:22\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{l}{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{s{\boldsymbol{L}}}=6.46{r}_{p}^{2}{{\boldsymbol{L}}}_{v}\sqrt{\mu {\rho }_{{sol}}\frac{\left|{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{pf}}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{ps}}\right|}{\left|{{\boldsymbol{L}}}_{v}\right|}}\\ {{\boldsymbol{L}}}_{v}=\left({{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{pf}}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{ps}}\right)\times \left[\nabla \times \left({{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{pf}}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{{ps}}\right)\right]\end{array}$$\end{document}FsL=6.46rp2LvμρsolVpf−VpsLvLv=Vpf−Vps×∇×Vpf−Vps

Both the wall-induced lift (FwL) and the shear-induced lift (FsL) involve the gradient operator (∇) in their respective formulations. To evaluate them, the first-order shape functions of the tetrahedral mesh are first determined, followed by the computation of their partial derivatives. The total lift force acting on the particle is then given by:23\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${F}_{L}={F}_{wL}+{F}_{{\boldsymbol{sL}}}$$\end{document}FL=FwL+FsL

The core idea of this method for generating particle trajectories is to predict the particle state at the next time step based on its current state and the forces acting on its surface. After the viscous force, net pressure, and lift force acting on the particle are obtained, respectively, according to Newton’s second law:24\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${F}_{vis}+{F}_{press}+{F}_{L}={m}_{p}\times a$$\end{document}Fvis+Fpress+FL=mp×awhere a is the acceleration of the particle and mp is the mass of the particle. The change in particle velocity during Δt is:25\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta {V}_{ps}=a\times Dt$$\end{document}ΔVps=a×Dt

Since Δt is very small, the particle displacement during this time is minimal. Therefore, it can be assumed that the particle surroundings remain unchanged over Δt, meaning that Fpress, FL, and Vrad_net are constant during this interval. Combining the above expressions, the particle velocity after Δt is:26\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{l}{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{ps}(t)=\left(\frac{{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{fix}}{{\zeta }_{stoke}}+{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{rad\_net}\right)\left(1-{e}^{-\frac{{\zeta }_{stoke}}{{m}_{p}}\times t}\right)+{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{ps}(0)\times {e}^{-\frac{{\zeta }_{stoke}}{{m}_{p}}\times t}\\ {\zeta }_{stoke}=6\pi \mu R\\ {{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{fix}={{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{press}+{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{L}\end{array}$$\end{document}Vps(t)=Ffixζstoke+Vrad_net1-e-ζstokemp×t+Vps(0)×e-ζstokemp×tζstoke=6πμRFfix=Fpress+FLwhere Vps(0) is the particle velocity at the beginning of the time step, and Vps(Δt) is the particle velocity after Δt. Integrating with respect to t, the displacement of the particle over this time interval can be expressed as:27\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\boldsymbol{s}}(t)=\left(\frac{{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{fix}}{{\zeta }_{stoke}}+{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{rad\_net}\right)t+\left[\left(\frac{{{\boldsymbol{F}}}_{fix}}{{\zeta }_{stoke}}+{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{rad\_net}-{{\boldsymbol{V}}}_{ps}(0)\right)\frac{m}{{\zeta }_{stoke}}\right]\left({e}^{-\frac{{\zeta }_{stoke}}{m}\times t}-1\right)$$\end{document}s(t)=Ffixζstoke+Vrad_nett+Ffixζstoke+Vrad_net-Vps(0)mζstokee-ζstokem×t-1

Assuming the particle’s center coordinate at the start of Δt is Scur, the coordinate at the next time step, Snext, is:28\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${S}_{next}={S}_{cur}+s(\Delta t)$$\end{document}Snext=Scur+s(Δt)

(1) Obtain the particle displacement vector Sdisp:29\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\boldsymbol{S}}}_{{\boldsymbol{disp}}}={S}_{next}-{S}_{cur}$$\end{document}Sdisp=Snext−Scur

(3) Assume that point G is the projection of point Q2 onto the collision wall surface. The displacement of the particle from point Q2 to point G can be expressed as:30\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta {\boldsymbol{s}}=G-{Q}_{2}=[({Q}_{1}-{Q}_{2})\cdot {{\boldsymbol{n}}}_{{\boldsymbol{wall}}}]{{\boldsymbol{n}}}_{{\boldsymbol{wall}}}$$\end{document}Δs=G−Q2=[(Q1−Q2)⋅nwall]nwall

(4) Adjust the predicted particle center coordinate:31\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${S}_{next}={S}_{next}+\Delta s$$\end{document}Snext=Snext+Δs

After extracting the recorded particle center positions from the experimental images, the simulation trajectories are mapped onto the coordinate system of the test images under the prerequisite that the micropillar array contours in both datasets are aligned. Using the particle center (xT,yT) in the test image as a reference point, we extract the corresponding coordinate (xT,yP) from the simulated trajectory. Since the simulation data points usually do not contain the exact value of xT, the two adjacent points enclosing xT on the predicted trajectory are used to estimate yP through linear interpolation. Finally, the Euclidean distance (DEuclidean) can be simplified as:32\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${D}_{Euclidean}=|{y}_{P}-{y}_{T}|$$\end{document}DEuclidean=|yP−yT|
